Title: The Delicate Balance of Special Autonomy Agreements: Lessons from Indonesia and the Philippines
Abstract: This paper examines whether asymmetric decentralisation can successfully reconcile demands for regional self-governance with the preservation of the nation-state. Through a comparative analysis of special autonomy arrangements in Aceh and Papua (Indonesia) and in Bangsamoro and the Cordillera Administrative Region (the Philippines), the study shows that asymmetric decentralisation often fails to deliver its theoretical promise when regional grievances are tied to deep poverty, perceptions of exploitation or long-standing civil conflict. The analysis identifies the number of veto players, central-state commitment, and the level of regional control over fiscal resources as key determinants of whether autonomy agreements mitigate conflict and economic deprivation. The cases reveal why autonomy arrangements may either stabilise restive regions or provoke new claims, and why economic inequalities often persist despite formal asymmetry.

Keywords: asymmetric decentralization, regional self-governance, conflict resolution, economic inequality, Indonesia, the Philippines

